@title Review: token bucket for the orders API
@subtitle Pull request 482, read with the code in view
@author Ana Ruiz
@date 2026-11-18

# What the change does

The limiter refilled a bucket only when a request was rejected, so a quiet client kept an empty bucket for ever. The fix refills on every call and adds a test for the idle case.

```diff
diff --git a/src/limiter.ts b/src/limiter.ts
--- a/src/limiter.ts
+++ b/src/limiter.ts
@@ -12,7 +12,8 @@ export class Limiter {
   allow(key: string): boolean {
     const b = this.buckets.get(key) ?? this.fresh();
-    if (b.tokens < 1) return false;
+    this.refill(b, Date.now());
+    if (b.tokens < 1) return this.reject(key);
     b.tokens -= 1;
     return true;
```

## The refill, line by line

Line 4 is the one to read twice: the elapsed time is clamped, so a clock that steps backwards cannot drain a bucket.

```ts lines
/** Adds the tokens earned since the last call, up to the bucket's capacity. */
private refill(b: Bucket, now: number): void {
  // A clock that steps backwards earns nothing.
  const elapsed = Math.max(0, now - b.last) / 1000;
  b.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, b.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
  b.last = now;
}
```

! Recommendation: Merge after the idle test lands; the change is small and the behaviour is now the documented one.

## The same idea in other places

The worker that drains the queue is Python and uses the same arithmetic.

```py
@dataclass
class Bucket:
    """A token bucket: `rate` tokens a second, at most `capacity`."""
    rate: float = 5.0
    capacity: int = 10

    def allow(self, now: float) -> bool:
        self.tokens = min(self.capacity, self.tokens + (now - self.last) * self.rate)
        return self.tokens >= 1  # the caller spends it
```

The limits live in configuration, and the dashboard reads the rejections from SQL.

```yaml
# limits per client tier
limiter:
  default: { rate: 5, capacity: 10 }
  partners:
    - name: "acme"
      rate: 50
```

```json
{"tier": "partners", "rate": 50, "capacity": 100, "burst": true, "notes": null}
```

```sql
SELECT client_id, count(*) AS rejected
FROM limiter_events -- one row per rejected call
WHERE kind = 'reject' AND at > now() - interval '1 hour'
GROUP BY client_id ORDER BY rejected DESC LIMIT 10;
```

```sh
# replay an hour of traffic against the branch
for f in logs/*.jsonl; do
  ./replay --rate=5 "$f" >> "${OUT:-replay.txt}"
done
```

## The status page, as Lede

The page that reports the limiter is written in Lede, and a fence can show its source.

````lede
@title Limiter status
# Rejections this week
data rej = day, calls; Mon, 120; Tue, 80; Wed, 45
chart bar rej title="Rejected calls" unit=calls
diagram path dir=LR
path: client "Client" user
path: api "Orders API" svc
path: client -> api : allowed | good
! Tip: A quiet day is the normal case; `0` is not an outage.
````

A fence in a language the compiler does not list is drawn as plain text, as before.

```text
limiter: 482 calls, 3 rejected, 0 errors
```
